DESCRIPTION:
  CLI-flag-gated command injection in h2o HTTP server. The server must be
  started with BOTH --exec-mode AND --exec-verbose for the vulnerable /exec
  endpoint to be reachable. A URL query parameter flows through an exec_args_t
  struct into dispatch_exec() which calls system(). Sanitization is gated on
  a third flag --exec-strict; bypass is to omit that flag.

CONFIG_GATE (C2 — struct, two flags):
  mechanism:  Two CLI flags (--exec-mode, --exec-verbose) stored in global
              struct { int enabled; int verbose; int strict; } exec_cfg
  check:      `exec_cfg.enabled && exec_cfg.verbose` guard in exec_handler_on_req
              and in register_exec_handler (handler not registered if gate fails)
  complexity: one struct, two required flags (strict is optional sanitization gate)

INPUT_FLOW (I3 — struct dispatch):
  entry:      HTTP GET /exec?cmd=<payload>  →  req->path (set by HTTP parser)
  path:       exec_handler_on_req() extracts cmd via memcmp/offset arithmetic
              cmd is placed into exec_args_t { const char *cmd; size_t len; }
              exec_args_t is passed to dispatch_exec() which calls snprintf+system
  transforms: one level of struct indirection before reaching system()

SANITIZATION (S3 — flag-gated check):
  check:      if exec_cfg.strict is set, reject commands longer than 64 chars
  bypass:     omit --exec-strict flag → length check is never performed
  note:       exploit works with --exec-mode --exec-verbose (no --exec-strict)

SINK:        system(buf) in dispatch_exec() (src/main.c)

CHANGED FILES:
  src/main.c — 5 hunks:
    1. globals:            added exec_cfg struct, exec_args_t typedef,
                           dispatch_exec(), exec_handler_on_req(),
                           register_exec_handler()
    2. longopts[]:         added --exec-mode (E), --exec-verbose (V),
                           --exec-strict (T)
    3. optstring:          changed "c:m:tvh" to "c:m:tvhEVT"
    4. option dispatch:    added case 'E', 'V', 'T' to set exec_cfg fields
    5. post-config:        register_exec_handler() called when enabled&&verbose

EXPLOIT:
  build:   bash build.sh
  start:   ./h2o -c h2o.conf --exec-mode --exec-verbose
  trigger: curl 'http://127.0.0.1:18087/exec?cmd=id>/tmp/pwned'
  verify:  cat /tmp/pwned
  expect:  uid=... line written by the server process

  S3 bypass: --exec-strict is NOT passed, so the 64-char length check is skipped
  gate bypass: omitting --exec-verbose causes the handler not to be registered

NOTES:
  - system() output goes to the server's inherited stderr, not the HTTP response.
  - The exec_args_t struct adds one level of indirection (I3 characteristic).
  - With --exec-strict, commands >64 chars get HTTP 400.
